  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 216 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge will continue to shift to our east and will \nbecome centered over the Lower Mississippi Valley and Gulf Coast \nstates late this week as an upper trough takes shape along the \nwestern CONUS. Temperatures will remain hot/above normal over our \nforecast area Thursday and Friday as we remain along the western \nperiphery of the ridge, though temperatures may trend a few degrees \nlower by Friday. Even so, highs will range in the mid 90s to around \n100 degrees over much of the region, and up to 102-108 degrees along \nthe Rio Grande. The chance for afternoon/evening showers and \nthunderstorms will increase over western portions of the forecast \narea late this week as weak shortwave impulses move over this \nportion of our region in the S-SW flow aloft between the ridge \ncentered to our east and the trough to our west. The latest forecast \nmaintains relatively good (30-60%) chances of convection over our \nwestern zones, especially over the Davis Mountains and the \nMarfa/Stockton Plateaus where storm chances will be highest. Other \nisolated to scattered (20-30% chance) showers and storms may develop \nover portions of the southeast New Mexico plains, Guadalupe \nMountains, Upper Trans Pecos, and western Permian Basin both days. \nDry conditions are still anticipated over the rest of the Permian \nBasin and Lower Trans Pecos in closer proximity to the ridge late \nthis week. A few storms that develop will be capable of producing \nlocally heavy rainfall, frequent lightning, and strong/gusty winds \nlate this week. The ridge will build back to the west and become \nstronger over west Texas and southeast New Mexico this weekend into \nearly next week. Storm chances decrease and mainly become confined \nto the higher terrain areas of the western CWA Saturday-Monday. \nAbove normal temperatures persist through the extended forecast \nperiod.",
